ow the  President to do whatever he wants to do  with any kind of oversight from Congress  or the courts which is really quite  shocking what got me into this I was  involved in litigation along with the  ACLU and the Center for Constitutional  Rights where an American citizen  attempted to prevent the President of  the United States for murdering him via  drone strike and the court in the  District of Columbia said I simply can't  supervise this sort of question it's a  political question reserved to the  president so when someone is saying I  have a constitutional right not to be  killed and the courts say while I'm  sorry I can't adjudicate that because  the president has decided that it's the  appropriate course of action you have to  ask yourself how we cross some sort of  fundamental threshold where we're now  not even just in a problem of  constitutional crisis but really have we  crossed over the normative order into a  state of affairs that's no longer  characterized by the rule of law is this  a situation where the president in this  case for example is somehow above the  law because the law refuses to take a  look at it's the same sort of problem  that brought about the rule of law in  the first place so in a 17th century  English king saying well of course I'm  subject to the law in that case it would  be God's law but no one can judge  whether or not in compliance with the  law so the United States has situation  where President Trump he sometimes runs  into problems because he'll say  explicitly I'm above the law no one can  stop me the courts can't stop me and  then you have courts pushing back a  little bit you have Congress pushing  back a little bit but in practice all he  does is act as if he's above the law the  courts are quite willing to say well  we're not fit to judge him we're not fit  to judge his cut his conduct in foreign  affairs or in defense of national  security and Congress does the same so  we have a practical situation that  whenever he says well this is an  exception it relates to national  security  everyone just acts as if he's above the  law I'd like you to explain the title a  permanent state of emergency unchecked  executive power and the demise of the  rule of law the demise of the rule of  law is directly related to the creation  of a permanent state of emergency so  after the 9/11 attacks in 2001 there was  a sense in Washington that something had  to be done the president had to be given  all these extensive powers to really get  down to business  Dick Cheney once they misleading as  having to work on the dark side and they  gave him that power with the idea that  well this was a temporary crisis we  would deal with the terrorists who  committed 9/11 bombing and now 16 years  later we have a civil war in Syria where  there's an organization known as Lou  Walsh um which is funded by the United  States and given weapons but in the  United States despite the fact that is  in fact a Syrian branch of al Qaeda so  the categories have become so amorphous  the grant of power to the President to  pursue whichever people he believes  they're his enemies at the moment I've  just been stretched so far that it can  cover literally anyone and I describe  that in detail in the book groups like  organizations in Somalia and Yemen are  now being designated as the effective  equivalent of the people who brought  down the World Trade Center and that's  allowing for a permanent state of  warfare which people like James Madison  said would destabilize the American  President but having governments  throughout history modern history anyway  have given themselves  excess power in a state of emergency I  mean we had the we had the war Measures  Act for example in which the Canadian  government for example in the Second  World War  practically controlled everything and  you know that was a state of emergency  and I'm just wondering given the current  political situation worldwide where we  do have such incredible d-- stability i  mean if they're not in some way a  permanent state of emergency in a way  because i mean you have these lone wolf  attacks you never know where it's coming  from the problem is that rather than  declaring war on a set of combatants or  even a particular terrorist organization  we've declared a war on terror which is  kind of like declaring a war on drugs or  war on poverty terrors like the poor it  will always be with us if we give the  president the power to continue as long  as there's going to be some sort of  terrorist threat we're effectively  saying well we need to abandon  democratic governance it was a failed  experiment we have situations also for  example in the situation with with  turkey where the president has given  himself extraordinary power is there is  there a thought in your mind this idea  of leaders acquiring more and more power  something that is sort of spreading i  mean could it possibly happen in places  like Canada Britain South Africa  Australia anywhere else like that  I do think so I think that we should  absolutely avoid complacency it would  have been unthinkable in the United  States after the reconstruction of the  rule of law post watergate that this  would occur so quickly there is a  tremendous appetite in the public for  just someone who can get things done cut  through all the problems of governance  and that person who comes forward like a  strongman saying I can solve all the  nation's problems is tremendously  attractive the problem is that it  denigrates the political process to the  point where well you have to ask  yourself do we believe in democracy at  all right now actually a Supreme Court  justice was speaking at a conference at  the University of Montreal on Tuesday  and she gave us the statistic that one  in six Americans state openly that they  prefer that America be ruled by its  military and that's really quite  shocking for a state founded on the  principle that it should be a government  